Fey is a specification for a novel markup language, designed to meet the
needs of individual expression. Some of the goals are for it to be easily
extensible via scripting and useable for a wide range of applications from
documentation to configuration files and data serialization.

Fey's core thesis is that markup should look the way we want it to, and so,
where possible, each syntactic feature offers a variety of markup options to
choose from, so far as the grammar permits it.

Some arbitrary choices are made that try to align with expected norms, but
otherwise Fey strives to break away from what one might consider 'standard'.


  I.A. Why scriptable

The experimental idea is that a Fey interpreter can read Fey instructions that
tell it how to interpret new Fey commands (and possibly syntax) that are defined
by the user. This should allow for the language to appear different to different
users, while still adhering to the same specification for a Fey interpreter.

This is where Fey gets its name from. In folklore and fantasy, a fey creature is
one that contains powerful magic, and is often unpredictable.

In the same way, Fey should be able to be reshaped and redefined by its users,
in a way that is unpredictable at first glance, but is well defined by the
instructions which define the additions and/or changes.

We don't know if all of these goals are mutually compatible, but until proven
otherwise we will exhuast all efforts to create a universally applicable markup
langauge.


  I.B. First steps

The current milestone goal is to expressly define the core syntax for fey to be
used in places where Markdown or Org-mode are currently, such as for README
documentation, writing articles for static site generation, or taking notes for
a PKM where the data in the notes is readily indexable for searching and
management.

In conjunction with that goal, a neovim plugin is being developed to make
editing fey files seamless, with features that are comparable to org-mode when
it comes to editing notes, such as moving heading subtrees up/down etc.

[url] https://github.com/Loreite/fey.nvim [Fey Markup Language Neovim Plugin]

Though do no expect for a very long time (if ever) that fey will have feature
parity when it comes to org-mode features like agenda and task tracking, unless
it becomes a clear point of interest for myself or large portion of the
community of users. If those features are currently important and/or
non-negotiable I encourage you to continue to use and support org-mode, and push
for its wider adoption.


  I.C. Why not Markdown or Org-mode

While these markup languages are incredibly valuable for different reasons:

Markdown is a simple specification widely adopted with a large support for
advanced features in different software such as obsidian or the Hugo static site
generator.

Org-mode is a more advanced markup ecosystem with features like an
agenda etc, and while very expressive with property drawers and keywords, they
also have limitations that make them unsuitable for use in Hugo for article
metadata, such as not having proper syntax for nested dictionaries or arrays of
values.

Ultimately, no matter where we turn, we have to give up some ground to use these
markup languages, as they don't cover all the purposes we have without bolting
on YAML frontmatter (a not so terrible solution) for example.

What Fey proposes to do is determine all the ground that has been lost to those
other languages and cover it explicitly while also filling the same roles they
are currently fit for. With everchanging needs in mind this is the reason for
Fey's goal for extensibility, to allow users to define their needs and adapt fey
to meet them where they are, if that goal is as possible as we imagine it to be.


  II. What does Fey look like

You're reading Fey right now. This document is written in standard Fey 0.0.2. As
are all other documents in the Fey repository.

Thus, if you want to get a feel for what kinds of things Fey can do out of the
box, then read on, and pay attention to the syntax and structure of all the
documents.


  III. How to use Fey

For now, this README is the only direct specification of Fey's syntax and features.


  IV. Documentation

Like most markup languages, Fey has a standard syntax for many features that make
writing up a large document easier, with features for headings, lists, tables, etc.


  IV.A. Headings

  IV.A.i. Segments, Tokens, Ordered Sets

You've already been reading the syntax for headings, but the formal definition
is that fey uses a '<token><delimiter>' (called a segment) notation for each
level of heading. Where 'token' is a string of characters that fall into one of
the following predefined ordered sets (or a set defined by the user):

    -  Capital Roman Numerals
    -  I, II, III, IV, V, VI, VII, VIII, IX, X ...

    -  Lowercase Roman Numerals
    -  i, ii, iii, iv, v, vi, vii, viii, ix, x ...

    -  Capital Letters
    -  A, B, C, ... X, Y, Z, AA, AB, AC, ...

    -  Lowercase Letters
    -  a, b, c, ... x, y, z, aa, ab, ac, ...

    -  Decimal Numbers
    -  1, 2, 3, ... 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, ...

    -  Hexadecimal Numbers
    -  0x1, 0x2, 0x3, ... 0xd, 0xe, 0xf, 0x10, 0x11, 0x12 ...

    -  Binary Numbers
    -  0b1, 0b10, 0b11, ... 0b1010, 0b1011, 0b1100, ...

Each ordered set is defined by its initial token of its ordering. So all
subsequent headings will be ordered at any given depth based on the first token
found at that depth on the page. Plugins should also be configurable to make
them only order at that depth for a given subtree.

(It is also possible to undefine an ordered set in order to free up a character)


  IV.A.ii. Delimiters, Anonymity, Prefix

The 'delimiter' is the separator between the tokens, the count of which
determines the depth or level of the heading. The tokens themselves only specify
information about the section ordering.

The 'delimiter' can also be defined by the user to be any character not found
in one of the ordered sets. The current set of delimiters is:

    . , : ; ! ? \ / ' " ` - + * = ~ ^ @ # $ & % [ ] ( ) { } < >

For any given heading segment, the token can be left empty or 'anonymous' (see
below). Altogether the sequence of segments is collectively called the heading
'signature'.


  IV.A.iii. All parts together

Headings are also defined by the specification to begin with two spaces before
the signature followed by a space and then the 'title' which is all the plain-text
until the end of the line.

The leading two spaces help the parser identify headings from standard lines of
text that begin like 'endofasentence. new sentence'. It also creates a clear
visual distinction for a reader of the plain text (when no syntax highlighting
is available) where a heading is relative to its body content.

Here are some examples of headings:

  .... Anonymous heading
  ..iii.. This is a partially anonymous heading
  IV.A..a. This is also a partially anonymous heading

  IV.A.iii.b. "'IV.i.A.b.' is the signature, but everything in quotes is the title"

  !!!! Note
    It is highly recommended that the use of an available Fey plugin or extension to
    your preferred text editor be used to automatically generate the correct heading
    syntax. Such a plugin should increment the ordering of the relevant level of
    heading for new headings, or update the ordering of all headings when a heading
    is moved or deleted.

    See link to fey.nvim above for neovim support.


  IV.B. Lists

Fey's ordered and unordered lists are as equally as expressive as headings,
using the same '<token><delimiter>' segment to partially define itself. See
seciton V.A.i. for more information on segments.

The second condition that defines a list is that the segment must be followed by
a mix of two spaces or tabs before the body content/paragraph.

Lists may be indented any number of spaces ex


  IV.B.i. Unordered lists

Unordered lists are are the most simple, an use an anonymous segment which
forgoes having a token:

    :  Each item in an unordered list is preceded by a character from a defined
       set, followed by a space.

        :  the standard defined set is { * + - = . }
    - The indentation of the start character determines the list level of the list item.

[Example]
See the lists above and below this example.


  IV.B.ii. Ordered lists

Ordered lists are a bit more complex than unordered lists, but still follow a simple syntax:

    - Each item in an ordered list is preceded the '.' character, followed by an value from an
        ordered set of characters (e.g. '1', 'a', 'A', 'i', 'I', '0x1', '0b1', etc.), followed by a space.
    - Or alternatively the ordered character is followed by ':', followed by a space.
    - The indentation of the '.' character determines the level of the list item.
    - The indentation of the list item is determined by the number of spaces or tabs before the '.' character.

    - any changes between sets of ordered characters from one list item to the next is considered a new list.
    - unless explicit ordering is maintained, such as 


[Example]
:1.  This is a top level ordered list item.
a: This is a second level ordered list item.
i: This is a third level ordered list item.
b: This is another second level ordered list item.
:2. This is another top level ordered list item.


  IV.C. Tags

Tags in Fey are a versatile component of Fey's syntax used for either syntactic
markup, structural markup, or data serialization.

All the pairs of '[], {}, (), <>' are considered the same interchangeable
syntactic unit in accordance with Fey's core thesis about user visual preference.

Line, Block, or paired Open/Close tags can be intermixed. That is, Block tags
can contain Line tags and Open/Close pairs, and Open/Close Pairs can contain
Line and Block tags. They all also contain at a minimum a 'name' and optionally
a list of 'metadata'.

The name can be any single word using these characters [a-zA-Z_-].

The metadata takes the form of a list of 'key: value' or 'key: "value with
spaces"' pairs separated by a ',' (comma).

The symbol (referred to as a tag's 'token') used to denote a Line/Block/Open/Close
tag can be any from the set [# @ & $ % : . / \ ? !]. In the table below we use
'#' (hash) as an example to show how the position of this token defines what
type of tag it is.

| Plain          | Line    | Block   | Open    | Close   |
|----------------+---------+---------+---------+---------|
| [generic text] | #[name] | [name]# | [name#] | [#name] |
| {generic text} | #{name} | {name}# | {name#} | {#name} |
| (generic text) | #(name) | (name)# | (name#) | (#name) |
| <generic text> | #<name> | <name># | <name#> | <#name> |
|----------------+---------+---------+---------+---------|


  IV.C.i. Plain Tags

The plain tag contains no name or metadata. It is defined as just a pair of
brackets, braces, parentheses, or chevrons with generic inner content that has no
set structure. This makes it available for parentheticals in paragraph text, or
to be used as part of other syntactic structures (see super-/sub-scripts).

Defining it this way allows Fey to refer back to text that is marked up this way
easily as a 'plain tag' elsewhere in this documentation.


  IV.C.ii. Line Tags

Line tags are non-nesting inline tags which consumes the text on the same line
after itself as its body up to the next occurence of its token or optionally
until the end of the line.

'''
Non body text #[name key: value, ...] body text# non-body text
Non body text #[name key: value, ...] body text until end of line
'''

The expcted format of the body text is defined by the name, eg. list or map.

Like with plain tags, the tag itself may span multiple lines.
See [ Data Serialization ] below for more information on lists.


  IV.C.iii. Block Tags

And then there is the nesting, whitespace indentation respecting tag such as
'#[]' which consumes both the rest of the line after itself as its head, and the
following lines of content which all begin at the next level of indentation as
its body, like so...
'''
[name key: value, ...]# head...
    body...
'''
where the expected head and body format are defined by the initial symbol, ie.
'#' which expects a map.
Like with plain tags, the tag itself may span multiple lines.
See [ Data Serialization ] below for more informiation on maps.


  IV.C.iv. Paired Open/Close Tags

Finally, we have nestable paired tags which are like plain tags in that they
only contain a name and metadata. They perform much like structural html or xml
tags, but the symbol used to define them determines what is expected in the body
which is all the content between each tag, and does not respect whitespace.

This adaptation allows fey to be easily translated into html, without needing to
support html embedding, as what would be html can be written directly using
paired tags, like so...

'''
[html/ lang: "en"]
[head/]
    [title/]Home[/title]
    [meta/ charset: "utf-8"]
    [style/]
        body {
            margin: 0%;
            padding: 0%;
            background-color: #221a0f;
        }
        hr {
            color: #ebc295;
        }
        .root {
            display: flex;
            flex-flow: row wrap;
            min-height: 100vh;
        }
    [/style]
[/head]
[body/]
    [header/]
    [/header]
    [div/ class: "root"]
        [div/ class: "menu"]
            [ul/]
                [li/][a/ href: "index.html"]
                        [h3/]Home[/h3]
                    [/a][/li]
                [li/][a/ href: "pages/character.html"]
                        [h3/]Character Sketch[/h3]
                    [/a][/li]
            [/ul]
        [/div]
        [div/ class: "page"]
            [div/ class: "content"]
                [div/ class: "text-content"]
                    [h1/]Home[/h1]
                    [h2/]Welcome[/h2]
                    [p/]
                        Neque, nisi vitae dapibus magna, suscipit eget sed parturient sit. Condimentum
                        nunc non molestie sed aliquam ac sed imperdiet ac. Eget, mi at tellus facilisis
                        tincidunt laoreet nam. Pulvinar risus habitasse varius pulvinar nec vivamus
                        imperdiet nulla bibendum. Eget ornare vivamus, porttitor porta non ante dui
                        lacus porttitor.
                    [/p]
                [/div]
                [div/ class: "footer"]
                    [img| src: "images/some-image.png",
                        height: "740", width: "580",
                        alt: "Nullam aenean, etiam imperdiet imperdiet velit.",
                        style: "display:block; margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;"]
                    [hr/]
                    [p/]Copyright (c) 2025 Aldo Zeroun[/p]
                [/div]
            [/div]
        [/div]
    [/div]
[/body]
[/html]
'''

  IV.D. Superscripts and Subscripts

To indicate superscripts, we can use '^' (caret) between the single 'carrier'
word or number and the superscript text or 'load'. If the carrier or load
contains an '_' (underscore) or the load contains multiple space delimited
words, numbers, or symbols, either side can be surrounded by a Plain Tag (see
Plain Tags) to remove ambiguity.

Similarly, subscripts use a '_' (underscore) between the single carrier and the
load. Plain tags can again be used to remove ambiguity if there are underscores
in the carrier or load, or the load contains multiple space delimited words,
numbers, or symbols.

'''
carrier^load 9.5^4 {10_000_000}^(the moon)
carrier_load 9.5_4 {10_000_000}_(the moon)
'''

Six settings are available to set whether Plain tags are required when using a
live renderer, on export to a staic format, or used by an editor to show pretty
wysiwyg text such as in neovim. When they are true, Plain tags are optional;
when they are false, Plain tags are required. By default they are all true.

    - fey-render-subscripts
    - fey-export-subscripts
    - fey-pretty-subscripts
    - fey-render-superscripts
    - fey-export-superscripts
    - fey-pretty-superscripts

Lastly, the plugin for your editor of choice should provide commands that can
toggle on/off showing wysiwyg super-/sub-scripts individually or together.

In the fey.nvim plugin for example we have:

    - FeyTogglePrettySuperscript
    - FeyTogglePrettySubscript
    - FeyPrettySubSuperscriptOn
    - FeyPrettySubSuperscriptOff


